11. Non-Functional Requirements
Category	Requirement
Performance	Pages and reading content should load efficiently; interactions should feel responsive under expected MVP load.
Security	Use secure authentication, authorization, and protected handling of user and publisher information.
Privacy	Collect only necessary data and provide transparent privacy and consent controls.
Scalability	Architecture should support growth in readers, publishers, books, and markets.
Availability	Readers should have reliable access to their purchased content, subject to service availability and rights.
Accessibility	Use readable typography, sufficient contrast, keyboard navigation, and accessible controls.
Compatibility	Support modern desktop and mobile browsers and responsive layouts.
Localization	Support multiple languages and a path toward regional currencies and formats.
Backup and recovery	Protect account, library, purchase, and publisher data with appropriate backup and recovery processes.
AI safety	Prevent unauthorized data access, clearly communicate uncertainty, and require confirmation for consequential actions.
Content protection	Apply appropriate copyright controls, access restrictions, and content protection measures.
Auditability	Record key administrative, payment, approval, and publishing actions.

12. Success Metrics and KPIs
KPI	Definition / purpose
Monthly Active Readers (MAR)	Unique readers active during a calendar month.
New Publisher Registrations	Number of new publisher accounts created.
Book Purchase Conversion	Percentage of eligible book detail visitors who complete a purchase.
Average Revenue per Reader	Revenue divided by active or paying readers, using a clearly defined period.
Book Completion Rate	Percentage of readers who reach the defined end of a book.
Chapter Discussion Participation	Percentage of eligible chapter completions followed by a discussion contribution.
StorySound Adoption	Percentage of active readers who use StorySound.
ROX Engagement Rate	Percentage of active users who interact with ROX.
Publisher Retention	Percentage of publishers who remain active over a defined period.
Repeat Purchase Rate	Percentage of purchasers who make another purchase within a defined period.

Proposed hypotheses for validation, not committed targets: 30% of active readers use StorySound; 20% participate in chapter discussions; 15% interact with ROX monthly; and 25% of first-time purchasers make another purchase within 30 days. Baselines and targets should be revised after user research and pilot data.
13. Risks, Dependencies, and Mitigations
Risk / dependency	Potential impact	Mitigation
Copyright and publishing rights	Unauthorized distribution or legal disputes.	Verify publisher rights and obtain appropriate agreements before publication.
Translation accuracy	Meaning, tone, or cultural context may be lost.	Prioritize publisher-approved translations and human review.
Payment fees and cross-border payouts	Low-priced books may have high relative transaction costs; payout complexity.	Validate payment providers, settlement rules, currencies, taxes, and pricing.
Publisher acquisition	Insufficient catalog reduces reader value.	Recruit publishers and secure quality content before broad reader acquisition.
Music licensing	Unlicensed tracks create legal exposure.	Use original, licensed, or appropriately royalty-free tracks with documented rights.
AI hallucination	ROX may invent book details or misstate analytics.	Ground responses in authorized source data and disclose uncertainty.
Community moderation	Spoilers, harassment, spam, and harmful content.	Provide reporting, moderation workflows, and community guidelines.
Content piracy	Unauthorized redistribution of digital books.	Assess DRM and access-control options appropriate to the business model.
Privacy and regulation	Non-compliance across jurisdictions.	Obtain legal review for privacy, consumer protection, tax, and digital-content obligations.

14. Open Questions and Decisions Required
Area	Question requiring stakeholder decision
Pricing	What is the regular price after the first seven days? Is the USD 2 launch price fixed across all markets?
Subscription	What does a subscription include: unlimited reading, monthly credits, exclusive content, discounts, or other benefits?
Revenue share	What percentage of each sale goes to the publisher and what fees or deductions apply?
Translation	Will NOVELIA support publisher-uploaded translations, AI translation, or both? Who approves translations?
Currencies and regions	Which launch countries, currencies, taxes, and payment methods are supported?
StorySound	Will tracks be selected by publishers, assigned from a curated library, or generated dynamically by AI?
Community	Are discussions public, limited to purchasers, or limited to readers who have completed the chapter?
Publisher verification	What documents and approval criteria are required for individuals and publishing companies?
Release scheduling	Can publishers schedule releases independently, or does every release require admin approval?
Reader analytics	What reader data may publishers access, and must analytics be aggregated or anonymized?
ROX autonomy	Which actions can ROX perform directly, and which require user confirmation?
Content removal	What happens to existing purchaser access when a publisher removes or loses rights to a book?

15. Prototype Acceptance Criteria
The initial interactive prototype is considered ready for stakeholder demonstration when the following conditions are met:
•	A user can enter the application using a reader, publisher, or admin demo role.
•	Reader and publisher navigation are visibly separated.
•	A reader can browse sample books, view details, and complete a simulated purchase.
•	A simulated purchased book appears in the reader's library.
•	A reader can open a sample book, navigate chapters, and retain reading progress during the demo.
•	StorySound can be switched on and off and plays an available demo track.
•	The Chapter Circle prompt appears after a sample chapter is completed, and the reader can post or skip.
•	ROX provides role-appropriate sample interactions and does not claim to execute unsupported actions.
•	A publisher can create or edit a sample book and view sample sales and earnings.
•	A publisher can submit a Reach Readers campaign request and see its status.
•	An administrator can review sample publisher, book, and campaign submissions.
•	The prototype clearly labels simulated purchases, sample analytics, and demo-only data.
•	Primary screens are responsive and important controls have functional interactions.
16. Assumptions and Constraints
•	The initial product is a responsive web application.
•	USD 2.00 for the first seven days and USD 4.00 thereafter are illustrative prototype assumptions only.
•	The revenue-share example of 80% publisher / 20% platform is illustrative only.
•	The first prototype may use mock data and local storage instead of a production backend.
•	No real payments or payouts are processed in the prototype.
•	StorySound uses a small set of approved demo tracks.
•	ROX may begin with mock responses before integration with a live AI model.
•	Production launch requires legal, security, privacy, payment, content-rights, and operational review.
17. Recommended Product Roadmap
Release	Focus	Included capabilities
Release 1 — MVP	Validate core marketplace and reading experience.	Reader and publisher access; discovery; book details; simulated purchases; library; reading progress; StorySound; Chapter Circle; basic ROX; publisher book management; basic analytics; admin approval.
Release 2 — Enhanced	Enable real operations and stronger engagement.	Payment integration; publisher payouts; approved multilingual editions; release scheduling; Reach Readers workflows; notifications; advanced analytics; expanded ROX actions.
Release 3 — Scale	Expand personalization and global reach.	AI-assisted translation; personalized recommendations; adaptive soundtracks; audiobooks; advanced reader insights; broader global distribution.

